{"id":"h267","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"An explorer, lost in. the desert, stumbled on an abandoned automobile whose gas tank still held 5 liters of fuel. Gasoline has a density of 0.67 g / cc. Assuming gasoline to be composed of C_7H_16 molecules, how many liters of water could he produce by burning the fuel and trapping the products ? The reaction is C_7H_16(l) + O_2(g) \\ding{217} CO_2(g) + H_2O(g)\nIs the correct answer \"12,060 g\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct mass of water is about 4.8 kg, not 12,060 g.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The fuel mass is 3350 g, from 5000 cm³ times 0.67 g/cm³.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The fuel amounts to about 33.4 mol of C7H16, using a molar mass of about 100.2 g/mol.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The balanced reaction is C7H16 + 11 O2 -> 7 CO2 + 8 H2O.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Burning the fuel yields about 267.5 mol of water, which is about 4820 g.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"At about 1 g/mL the water volume is roughly 4.8 L.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The 12,060 g figure cannot be reproduced from the fuel mass, molar masses, or stoichiometry.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The CO2 mass of about 10,300 g does not match 12,060 g either.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Because the products are trapped, the water is condensed and can be reported as a liquid volume.","confidence":0.7,"lean":-1,"theta":1.0996,"phi":-3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.8,"rel